  "summary": {
    "en": {
      "caveats": "The findings are based on qualitative ethnographic fieldwork within a specific South American city, which may limit generalizability to vertical housing contexts with different socioeconomic or regulatory conditions.",
      "what": "Based on a year-long ethnographic study of high-rise buildings in Santiago, Chile, this research examines how residents use messaging applications to establish localized digital economies. These informal, neighbor-to-neighbor commercial transactions foster social interactions and community relations within highly dense, vertical residential environments.",
      "why": "It demonstrates that digitalized consumption practices can actively counter the social isolation and impersonal nature typically associated with high-rise living, showing how rigid architectural structures are socially reshaped through everyday digital neighborliness."
